====================================================================================================
IS THE READOUT THE QUANTUM PART?  QLTO vs SPSA at matched TOTAL shots
====================================================================================================
  Same radius R = 0.45 for both, so neither carries a bias the other does not.
  SPSA charged 2G circuits per sigma sample, QLTO G per block; both spend
  the same TOTAL shots. Scored by cos(g_hat, grad E). 8 repeats.

    N   M   T total   cos QLTO   cos SPSA   1-cos QL   1-cos SP   ratio   winner
  ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    4  24      8192     0.7846     0.7685    0.21540    0.23149    1.07     QLTO
    4  24     32768     0.9412     0.8280    0.05880    0.17201    2.93     QLTO
    4  24    131072     0.9723     0.8433    0.02768    0.15666    5.66     QLTO
  ..............................................................................
    6  36      8192     0.6371     0.6420    0.36285    0.35804    0.99      tie
    6  36     32768     0.8706     0.7629    0.12941    0.23710    1.83     QLTO
    6  36    131072     0.9634     0.8095    0.03658    0.19052    5.21     QLTO
  ..............................................................................

  'ratio' is SPSA error over QLTO error: above 1 means the bounded readout
  is winning. The claim under test is that it wins AND that the margin
  grows with M, since the term it removes is |grad E|^2 - (d_iE)^2 and that
  is what grows. A flat or inverted ratio says the superposition and the
  readout both buy nothing, and this family of in-circuit optimisers is
  classical stochastic approximation with extra steps.
